Bound the interpreter by instructions executed.

`wasm-ctor-eval` has no way to stop: a ctor that loops forever loops forever,
and the command line tool relies on its caller killing the process. An embedder
has nothing to kill.

The two limits that exist do not serve. `maxLoopIterations` counts per entry to
a loop, and reports a non-constant flow rather than a host limit -- that is
`precompute`'s mechanism, not a budget. `maxDepth` does call `hostLimit`, but
bounds only recursion.

`ExpressionRunner::visit` is the one place every instruction passes through, and
it is where `maxDepth` is already checked, so the counter goes beside it. It
cannot be added from `wasm-ctor-eval.cpp` alone: `EvallingModuleRunner` is a CRTP
subclass, but the base calls `visit` unqualified on itself, so an override there
does not see the base's own calls.

Exceeding it is a host limit, and `wasm-ctor-eval` already overrides `hostLimit`
to throw `FailToEvalException` -- so the ctor that ran over fails, and the ones
before it keep what they evalled. That is strictly better than a timeout, which
loses the whole run.

The count is over the life of the runner, not per call, so it bounds the work a
caller can be made to do rather than the shape of one function. Zero, the
default, is `NO_LIMIT` and nothing changes.

--- a/src/wasm-interpreter.h
